  "image_name": "8f987d2ea525d525724e67e964094e6c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares electrochemical measurement setups and how electrode geometry affects what is actually being measured. In the top row, three beaker schematics show a potentiostat/galvanostat connected to working, reference, and counter electrodes. The associated circuit models illustrate how impedance is split among solution resistance, charge-transfer resistance, double-layer capacitance, and diffusion-like elements. With a three-electrode arrangement, the reference electrode measures potential in a way that helps separate the working-electrode and counter-electrode contributions. In simpler two-electrode-like situations, those contributions become lumped together. When the counter electrode is made much larger, its own impedance becomes relatively small, so the overall response is dominated by the working electrode and electrolyte path, giving a simplified equivalent circuit.\n\nThe bottom row applies this idea to implanted electrodes in tissue. One sketch places the counter electrode as an external bone screw, while another places it directly on the implant. The comparison indicates a tradeoff: an external counter electrode reduces the counter electrode’s influence but increases tissue impedance, whereas an on-implant counter electrode lowers tissue impedance but causes the counter electrode to contribute more strongly to the measured signal. Overall, the figure explains how measurement configuration shapes interpretation of electrochemical impedance data.",
